(In presenting the British public with the following pages, containing a) Tj T*
(brief sketch of my life and travels, together with a description of the) Tj T*
(customs and present condition of my native land, I am actuated solely by) Tj T*
(motives which, I trust, a careful perusal of this work will prove to be) Tj T*
(disinterested.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All nations are more or less patriotic; none more so than the inhabitants) Tj T*
(of the British isles.  With them the inducements to this love of home are) Tj T*
(all-sufficient, for their religion is the purest, their government and) Tj T*
(laws the best in the world, and they are second to no people in the) Tj T*
(enjoyment of privileges and blessings, such as could be only enjoyed by a) Tj T*
(?peculiar people,? under the immediate protection of the Almighty) Tj T*
(Benefactor.  Next to them we may rank, as promoters of freedom and) Tj T*
(enlightenment, the citizens of the United States, those other scions of a) Tj T*
(noble stock.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Yet so peculiar is that innate love of man for the particular country and) Tj T*
(people with which are associated the early years of his childhood, that) Tj T*
(even the son of utter darkness, born and bred a savage, inured to every) Tj T*
(hardship and privation, who boasts of no city, scarcely professes a) Tj T*
(religion, whose home is the desert waste, his bed the warm sands of) Tj T*
(Arabia, even he, the wild Bedouin, in his untutored heart, sets boundless) Tj T*
(store by the place and people to which early attachment has rivetted his) Tj T*
(affections.  Separate him from these and from his beloved mare, and no) Tj T*
(riches or pleasures could compensate him for the loss.  This is also) Tj T*
(applicable to the humble and oftentimes oppressed natives who dwell in) Tj T*
(the towns and villages of Syria, Lebanon, and Palestine.  Though for) Tj T*
(centuries they have been subjected to the heavy yoke of bondage, and of) Tj T*
(late years, like the Israelites of old, were bondsmen to Egypt; however) Tj T*
(much they may have deplored their hard fate, none have ever dreamt of) Tj T*
(quitting the dear land of their forefathers?those ancestors who were) Tj T*
(coeval with the patriarchs.  Some till the ground where Abraham once) Tj T*
(tended his flocks; others cut timber where the men of Hiram and Solomon) Tj T*
(once hewed cedars for the temple at Jerusalem; but the boast and glory of) Tj T*
(all these is, that they dwell in the land where the Promise was) Tj T*
(fulfilled.  One may be by birth a Nazarene, another a townsman of Cana.) Tj T*
(A day or two?s journey enables him to reach that very Bethlehem where the) Tj T*
