() Tj T*
(Apart from these cherished associations of the spiritual with the) Tj T*
(temporal world, the native of the Holy Land is fondly attached to his) Tj T*
(country, because its climate is congenial to his manners, its soil) Tj T*
(productive, its inhabitants hospitable, its waters the purest, air the) Tj T*
(freshest, sun the brightest, fruits the most delicious, and flowers the) Tj T*
(sweetest and most wildly profuse.  All these gifts in the greatest) Tj T*
(luxuriance are to be found within the Lebanon range?that Lebanon of which) Tj T*
(the inspired bard, the wisest of men and the best of kings, sings in his) Tj T*
(beautiful metaphor on Christian love. {3}  ?Thy plants are an orchard of) Tj T*
(pomegranates with pleasant fruits. . .  A fountain of gardens, a well of) Tj T*
(living waters, and streams from Lebanon.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With such a past to dwell on, it is not surprising that the poor,) Tj T*
(neglected peasant of Syria may still proudly vaunt himself of his) Tj T*
(birthright and country.  I, too, hope, kind reader, for your sympathy in) Tj T*
(my sharing this national characteristic, and for endeavouring, as far as) Tj T*
(in me lies, to promote the welfare, both temporal and eternal, of my) Tj T*
(fellow countrymen and native land.  The former, alas! are gradually) Tj T*
(sinking deeper and deeper into the meshes of superstition and idolatry;) Tj T*
(the latter groans under a heavy yoke, rendered still less supportable by) Tj T*
(the grossest ignorance.  The indefatigable propagators of the Romish) Tj T*
(faith are arousing the people from their pristine ignorance, only, I) Tj T*
(fear, to plunge them into a more fearful vortex of errors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I rush to the rescue; for God has blessed me far above my countrymen, by) Tj T*
(shedding the true light of the Gospel around my pathway, through the) Tj T*
(instrumentality of good and holy men, whom He has chosen for His especial) Tj T*
(service, and who have bestowed on me the priceless boon of a Christian) Tj T*
(education.  I am willing and anxious to devote every hour of my life, and) Tj T*
(all my poor means, to the furtherance of His cause.  Yet, though much may) Tj T*
(combine in my favour, I am inadequate to the accomplishment of the good I) Tj T*
(desire for my country, without the aid, wise counsel, and support of the) Tj T*
(Christian inhabitants of Great Britain.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Reader! in the following pages I have endeavoured to depict as clearly as) Tj T*
(I can the evil and the remedy.  I have glanced over the leading features) Tj T*
(of my life, to show how circumstances, trivial in themselves, appear to) Tj T*
(have combined in my favour, that I should be an humble instrument in the) Tj T*
(hands of my Maker, to work out a brighter and better hope for dear Syria.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That ?pearl of great price,? pure Christianity, has been cherished and) Tj T*
(nurtured within these isles till the true faith has reared itself up like) Tj T*
(a mighty mirror, reflecting the glorious light of the blessed truths of) Tj T*
(the Gospel far and wide.  May one beam of charity, reflected from thence,) Tj T*
(alight upon the mother church of Syria?that church now sunk in misery and) Tj T*
(degradation, but from which \(remember, O Christian of Great Britain\) was) Tj T*
