(CHAPTER I.) Tj T*
(SCENES OF EARLY CHILDHOOD.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(My earliest recollections are associated with the lovely and rural) Tj T*
(village of Shuay-fât, my birth-place, on the Lebanon; and where, if not) Tj T*
(the happiest, certainly the most innocent years of my childhood were) Tj T*
(passed.  My late father had no fixed residence at that place, but he,) Tj T*
(with the rest of his family, usually resorted there to spend the summer) Tj T*
(months and part of the autumn and spring.  In winter the cold became) Tj T*
(intense, owing to the elevated position of the village; consequently most) Tj T*
(of its inhabitants and summer visitors, including amongst these latter my) Tj T*
(own family, invariably wintered at Beyrout.  My uncle, Sheikh Faris) Tj T*
(Biridi, filled the important and respected post of _katib_, or secretary) Tj T*
(to the Emir Beshir Shahab, the late prince of Lebanon, who resided at the) Tj T*
(village of Deyr-al Kamar, situated a few hours? journey from Shuay-fât.) Tj T*
(At least three times a week my uncle?s duties compelled him to visit the) Tj T*
(Emir.  Sheikh Faris was universally respected amongst the villagers; his) Tj T*
(house was the best?his grounds the most extensive, and he himself in) Tj T*
(reality, an intelligent and well-informed man.  For a Syrian, he was) Tj T*
(deeply read and well skilled in the use of his pen; but above all, he was) Tj T*
(an earnest and devout Christian, a kind father, and a good friend?virtues) Tj T*
(which gained for him the esteem and love of all the neighbouring) Tj T*
(villagers, as well Moslems and Druses as the Christians.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Under the favourable auspices of this kind man?s tuition, I first learned) Tj T*
(to read and write my native tongue; and, as I was afterwards informed,) Tj T*
(even at that early age, gave cheering proofs of an active mind, and) Tj T*
(evinced an aptitude and love for the acquirement of knowledge.  I could) Tj T*
(not possibly have had a better guide, both as regards precept and) Tj T*
(example.  So long as I remained under his hospitable roof, his great and) Tj T*
(chief care was to richly stock my young mind with doctrines well adapted) Tj T*
(to promote the welfare of the soul in after years on all important) Tj T*
(business.  His household arrangements were an example for others.  He was) Tj T*
(an early riser himself, and insisted on all his household following this) Tj T*
(healthful practice: his maxim was that sleep was for the dark hours of) Tj T*
(the night?work and recreation for the light?prayers and thanksgivings for) Tj T*
(all seasons.) Tj T*
