(My uncle was accustomed when at home to repair every morning, during the) Tj T*
(spring and summer seasons, to the top of a neighbouring hill, which) Tj T*
(commanded a view over an extensive range of country.  On these occasions) Tj T*
(it was my wont to accompany him.  A servant preceded us carrying a small) Tj T*
(carpet and a cushion or two; I carried my uncle?s pipe and tobacco-pouch) Tj T*
(with flint, steel, and tinder, in one hand; in the other, the Kitab) Tj T*
(Mukaddas, or Arabic Bible, printed in England, by the Church Missionary) Tj T*
(Society.  As soon as my uncle had seated himself, and assumed his pipe,) Tj T*
(he would make me sit at his feet and read out to him from the good Book,) Tj T*
(illustrating and commenting as opportunity occurred.  The hundred and) Tj T*
(fourth Psalm, than which none could be better suited to the time and) Tj T*
(place, was usually his favourite.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From our elevated position, we could command a view, not only of our own) Tj T*
(dearly cherished and beautiful hamlet, but also of many of the) Tj T*
(surrounding villages.  At our feet lay Shuay-fât, with its neat little) Tj T*
(cottages and cleanly swept court-yards, surrounded by a dense little) Tj T*
(forest of mulberries, oranges, lemons, apricots, olives, countless vines,) Tj T*
(and many other fruits; the dark leaves of an occasional poplar lending) Tj T*
(variety to the beauty and shading of the foliage.  Not a man, woman, or) Tj T*
(child, moved to and fro in the narrow little streets, but their names and) Tj T*
(occupations were well known to us.  The dogs wagged their tails in happy) Tj T*
(recognition of my shrill sharp whistle, and a thousand echoes caught up) Tj T*
(the signal.  The verdant hills and valleys that surrounded us were) Tj T*
(thickly dotted with cattle and sheep contentedly browsing upon the rich) Tj T*
(pasturage.  Peeping over the densely wooded plantations, the tops of the) Tj T*
(little whitewashed houses pointed out the locality of some well-known) Tj T*
(village.  Clear streams of water sparkling in the glowing sunlight, often) Tj T*
(intersected the plains and valleys, or rushed headlong down the steep) Tj T*
(sides of some deep dell, abounding with wild flowers and myrtle bushes.) Tj T*
(Far below, where the distant fields in square patches of variegated hues,) Tj T*
(green bespangled with blue and crimson flowers; sometimes covered, like a) Tj T*
(sheet of pure gold, with countless buttercups, and sometimes in) Tj T*
(uncultivated patches of sombre brown; but what I most dearly loved to) Tj T*
(gaze at was the broad blue sea in the distance, looking so pleasantly) Tj T*
(cool and calm, with here and there a patch of deeper blue, where the) Tj T*
(breeze sportively ruffled the waves.  I always thought of Nabiy Yunas {8}) Tj T*
(and the great fish, and wondered if many such fish were yet taking their) Tj T*
(pastime in the deep.  How little I imagined at that time that I was) Tj T*
(destined to traverse those mighty waters, and to suffer myself to be) Tj T*
(borne away on their waves hundreds of miles from shore, exposed to raging) Tj T*
(tempests in a fragile bark!  Such a notion would then have been scouted) Tj T*
(by all my friends; and I myself should have been foremost in deriding the) Tj T*
(idea, and in opposing, that which has since proved conducive to my best) Tj T*
(interests, temporal, and I trust eternal; but I was then a child, and) Tj T*
(understood and acted as a child.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From this pleasant spot, my uncle gazed with rapture upon the surrounding) Tj T*
(scenery, as the first rays of the sun peered above the snow-capped peaks) Tj T*
(of lofty Lebanon, and spread a golden mantle over the vast panorama; from) Tj T*
(my childhood, I have known how to appreciate the beauties of nature in) Tj T*
(all their poetry; and I admire them still, but with a milder and more) Tj T*
