(the birds make their nests.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Thus profitably and pleasantly the early hours of the day would be) Tj T*
(consumed.  I was then dismissed with sage advice, to remember throughout) Tj T*
(the day what I had read and heard; and my uncle being called away by his) Tj T*
(avocations, I was left to amuse myself with my play-mates in the village,) Tj T*
(until the hour of noon summoned us to our substantial mid-day meal.  Like) Tj T*
(most boys, we were prone to mischief.  I remember a favourite game) Tj T*
(amongst the village lads, which occasionally terminated in a squabble,) Tj T*
(and was known by the name of Al Cadi, or The Judge.  The Cadi was chosen) Tj T*
(by lot, as were the officers of his court, and the imaginary plaintiffs) Tj T*
(and defendants.  Squatted on the ground, under the pleasant shade of some) Tj T*
(mulberry-tree, we then held a court.  Sentence was recorded and executed;) Tj T*
(and sometimes the boy who personated the imaginary criminal was sentenced) Tj T*
(to be bastinadoed.  On these occasions, the executioners laid about them) Tj T*
(so smartly with the light switches of the mulberry and olive, that though) Tj T*
(the boy?s shoes were never removed, the lash penetrated to the sole of) Tj T*
(the foot, and then the pretended culprit, smarting from pain, would lose) Tj T*
(all command over his temper; a _melée_ would ensue, which outraged the) Tj T*
(dignity of the court, and usually terminated by all the members, the Cadi) Tj T*
(included, being summarily whipped for their naughtiness.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When the hour of mid-day was announced by the striking of gongs, which in) Tj T*
(Syria are usually substituted for bells at some churches, all our family) Tj T*
(assembled for _futar_, and my uncle would enter, followed by the peasants) Tj T*
(employed about his plantations, together with his other servants.  This) Tj T*
(was the signal for the cook and her assistant to carry into the centre of) Tj T*
(the yard a large iron cauldron, containing the _ruzz-mufalfal_, or) Tj T*
(whatever was prepared for the day for the supply of the whole household.) Tj T*
(Clean shining platters were ranged in piles round this cauldron, and a) Tj T*
(blessing having been first asked, the food was ladled out?a goodly) Tj T*
(portion for each?enough and no waste.  The only distinguishing mark at) Tj T*
(this family meal was, that the members of my uncle?s family were all) Tj T*
(seated round a low circular table, and reclined upon carpets and against) Tj T*
(cushions.  The others sat where their fancy dictated; but they chiefly) Tj T*
(crowded under that side of the court-yard wall which afforded a shade) Tj T*
(from the heat of the sun.  In addition to the contents of the cauldron,) Tj T*
(there was generally a dish of stewed meat and vegetables; or \(if the) Tj T*
(season was Lent\), of the egg-vegetable, or _batinjan_, and the) Tj T*
(vegetable-marrow, sliced and fried in oil?with as many cucumbers,) Tj T*
