(father?s resources had been sadly crippled by the piratical affair;) Tj T*
(besides, I was of an age when youths in Syria earn their own livelihood,) Tj T*
(and my education was sufficiently advanced to enable me to enter upon the) Tj T*
(duties of life.  I could read and write my own language; and this was all) Tj T*
(that was expected, and much more than many youths of my age could boast.) Tj T*
(I had no thought then of acquiring a knowledge of foreign languages.  To) Tj T*
(escape from the thraldom of school is always a source of great delight to) Tj T*
(schoolboys.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As far as my own views went, I was bent upon going to Damascus; and) Tj T*
(though my dear parents opposed this wish at first, I gradually coaxed) Tj T*
(them into a consenting mood; and perhaps the greatest inducement for them) Tj T*
(to yield to my wishes, was the fact of our having a wealthy and) Tj T*
(influential friend, then residing at Damascus, who had been a) Tj T*
(fellow-_katib_ of my uncle?s, and who occupied a high post in the service) Tj T*
(of the Pasha.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To this worthy man?s care I was confided; and, taking leave of my dear) Tj T*
(parents, and accompanied by their blessing, I left Beyrout, and proceeded) Tj T*
(to Damascus; a city which existed before the patriarch Abraham?s time,) Tj T*
(being referred to as a well-known place, in Gen. xiv.  It was the chief) Tj T*
(city of Syria, founded by Rezin, and was sacked by Jeroboam II., king of) Tj T*
(Israel.  It is still a comparatively thriving and populous city, and has) Tj T*
(those natural resources of climate, soil, and abundance of water, which) Tj T*
(cannot fail to perpetuate its fame as the garden of the East.  Here,) Tj T*
(shortly after my arrival, I was fortunate enough, through the influence) Tj T*
(of our friend, to procure a lucrative and rising situation.  At this) Tj T*
(place I remained a considerable time, delighted with its climate and) Tj T*
(beauty, as also well pleased with my office and with my associates.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(No pen can give an adequate idea of the delights of Damascus.  The) Tj T*
(nearest approach I can hope to make to a truthful description, will be) Tj T*
(simply to depict what I saw and experienced; and this perhaps will give) Tj T*
(the stranger a better conception of the place than the flowery rhapsodies) Tj T*
