(Arrived at Lattakia, we became the guests of the hospitable brothers) Tj T*
(Elias.  Signor Mosi Elias is the British vice-consul at that port; and) Tj T*
(seldom have I had the happiness of meeting with a more worthy man; but,) Tj T*
(in fact, the whole of his family are distinguished for their great) Tj T*
(courtesy and hospitality to all strangers.  This eulogy may fairly be) Tj T*
(extended to all the native agents established along the sea-coast of) Tj T*
(Syria; although, unfortunately, their humble efforts are not always duly) Tj T*
(appreciated.  English gentlemen, accustomed to every comfort and luxury) Tj T*
(that wealth can command, little imagine the expense and trouble incurred) Tj T*
(by many of the humble Syrian agents in their efforts to afford) Tj T*
(hospitality to British travellers.  Receiving no salary, and yet) Tj T*
(compelled to maintain a certain position to support the dignity of) Tj T*
(office, the means in their power must necessarily be limited; but as far) Tj T*
(as house-room goes?a bed, a dinner, breakfast, and supper, according to) Tj T*
(their limited means; these are always cheerfully offered to the) Tj T*
(traveller; and the poor consular agent, who has almost insuperable) Tj T*
(difficulties to contend with, so as to enable him to impress the local) Tj T*
(authorities with a due sense of the importance and respectability of his) Tj T*
(office, is glad to avail himself of the opportunity of having an) Tj T*
(Englishman as guest under his roof, to convince the neighbours and his) Tj T*
(fellow-townsmen of his influence with the British.  I have known) Tj T*
(instances where a poor consular agent has even parted with some valuable) Tj T*
(family relic, so as to enable him to afford a hearty welcome to some) Tj T*
(Englishman of distinction; while, perhaps, the only return he has met) Tj T*
