(Asiatic origin.  Our great cause of surprise, is the sudden rise of) Tj T*
(individuals in comparatively indigent circumstances to places of great) Tj T*
(power.  When, however, it is considered that the Orientals view the) Tj T*
(various grades of society in another light to the Western Europeans, the) Tj T*
(sudden aggrandisement of individuals from the lower classes will cease to) Tj T*
(be a matter of surprise.  In Turkey, men of the noblest birth mix) Tj T*
(indiscriminately with all ranks, and he who is possessed of wealth,) Tj T*
(talent, or interest, may rise to offices of the greatest trust; and, as) Tj T*
(?knowledge is power,? I can see no reason why talent should not be) Tj T*
(brought into the notice which it merits.  As a proof of the justice and) Tj T*
(benefit accruing from this system, I may adduce the case of a Kapudan) Tj T*
(Pasha, whose station in life was very humble, but, being gifted with more) Tj T*
(than ordinary abilities, he was promoted to the chief command of the) Tj T*
(Turkish fleet, which was never better managed than whilst under his) Tj T*
(control.  Other instances of a similar character are of frequent) Tj T*
