(servants, and practise other extortions.  To meet these urgent exactions,) Tj T*
(the poor villagers are obliged annually to raise loans guaranteed on the) Tj T*
(ensuing season?s crops at a most usurious rate of interest, as high as) Tj T*
(from twenty-five to thirty-five and forty per cent. per annum, either) Tj T*
(from wealthy Jews, Armenians, or Greeks, and formerly even many of the) Tj T*
(protegés of the different European consulates took advantage of this) Tj T*
(state of things, and fattened upon the misfortunes and miseries of the) Tj T*
(poor peasants, over whom they rode roughshod.  The existence of so) Tj T*
(terrible an evil could not long remain unknown to the inquiring mind of) Tj T*
(the Sultan, and though his sources of correct information have) Tj T*
(necessarily been meagre, he acquired an insight into it, sufficient to) Tj T*
(convince him of the necessity for a change.  Accordingly, he ordered) Tj T*
(certain taxes to be abolished, others to be reduced; and he, above all,) Tj T*
(is endeavouring to organise an honest and simple system of collection.) Tj T*
(To this end all his ministers and employés have been obliged, before) Tj T*
(taking office, to promise, upon oath, to discharge their several duties) Tj T*
(impartially and justly; above all, not to receive bribes in any shape.) Tj T*
(He has been foiled to a great extent in these attempts; and hence may be) Tj T*
(derived the clearest and simplest explanation of the financial) Tj T*
(embarrassments of his government.  _Apropos_ of this, I may quote from) Tj T*
(the letter of a friend, which has just come to hand.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(?Everybody seems to imagine that the speedy downfall of Turkey is) Tj T*
(inevitable, that its doom is all but sealed, and that she is passing as) Tj T*
(rapidly as she can into the hands of Russia.  But it ought to be well) Tj T*
(known in Western Europe, that the so-much-talked-of balance of power in) Tj T*
(the East, cannot be thus so easily or so recklessly sacrificed by the two) Tj T*
(great powers, England and France.  The jealousy of these powers is a) Tj T*
(sufficient safeguard for Turkey; and they will protect her from any) Tj T*
(aggression on the part of Russia or Austria on her rights and territory;) Tj T*
(and it is to me evident that Russia?s long course of policy with regard) Tj T*
(to the Ottoman empire in Turkey, will be frustrated from a quarter whence) Tj T*
(she may least expect it.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
