(Resuming my narrative, my readers will be interested by a slight sketch) Tj T*
(of Egypt.  This country, now called by the natives ?Messir,? was styled,) Tj T*
(in the Hebrew Scriptures, ?The land of Mizraim??a strange similarity in) Tj T*
(the two names, which places it beyond a doubt that, however much the face) Tj T*
(of the country may have been changed since the days of Moses and the) Tj T*
(children of Israel, and though consecutively under the sway of) Tj T*
(governments and people whose language and dialects varied in the extreme,) Tj T*
(the same original name has been faithfully preserved, though corrupted) Tj T*
(and abbreviated by various pronunciations given to it by various people.) Tj T*
(A land of troubles and misery it has been through many long centuries,) Tj T*
(from the fearful days when Aaron?s rod manifested the supreme power of) Tj T*
(the God of Abraham before the eyes of an unbelieving and stiff-necked) Tj T*
(people, down to within the last few years.  The frightful devastations) Tj T*
(committed by the plague, and the extermination of the Mameluke power;) Tj T*
(these have been the last manifest outpourings of the wrath of God.  Let) Tj T*
(us hope that the full cup of indignation has been poured out and emptied) Tj T*
(to the dregs; and that the prophetic words of Isaiah have been fulfilled) Tj T*
(as far as regards the curse, and that the predicted blessing is about to) Tj T*
(fall upon the land.  ?The Lord shall smite Egypt: he shall smite and heal) Tj T*
(it; and they shall return to the Lord, and he shall heal them,? etc.) Tj T*
(\(Isaiah xix. 22?25\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The striking allusion made to the fertility of the soil of Egypt in Gen.) Tj T*
(xli. 47??The earth brought forth by handfuls??is still exemplified by the) Tj T*
(produce.  Corn is so plentiful, that cargoes are annually shipped for the) Tj T*
(maintenance of other lands, and when the famine was sorely felt in the) Tj T*
(neighbouring countries, whole fleets of vessels, laden with corn from) Tj T*
(Alexandria, brought to England timely succour to starving multitudes, and) Tj T*
(enriched the coffers of not a few speculative merchants, who made the) Tj T*
(miseries of their fellow-beings a means of advancing their own welfare in) Tj T*
(the world.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is little doubt but that Egypt has made great strides in) Tj T*
(civilisation under the sway of the present enlightened viceroy; for we) Tj T*
(have daily evidence of her continued improvement.  Abbas Pasha is now) Tj T*
(only about forty-five years of age; he is the son of the eldest son of) Tj T*
(Mahomet Ali Pasha, and, therefore, according to the Egyptian rule, which) Tj T*
(gives precedence to the brother or his children, became entitled to the) Tj T*
(throne after the decease of Ibrahim, whose children, in some countries,) Tj T*
(would have been considered lawful successors.  Abbas Pasha, unlike his) Tj T*
(predecessor, whose habits greatly contributed to curtail his life, is a) Tj T*
(man of very moderate and temperate style of living; he has but one wife,) Tj T*
(and, by this lady, an only son, now about twelve years of age.  At the) Tj T*
(recommendation of the honorable Mr. Murray, the late British) Tj T*
(consul-general in Egypt, the viceroy sent to England to engage a tutor) Tj T*
(for the education of this son in English, and Mr. Artin, an English) Tj T*
(lawyer, was the lucky individual fixed upon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(No sooner had Mr. Artin arrived in Egypt, than Abbas Pasha promoted him) Tj T*
(to the dignity of Bey, and he now ranks amongst the nobles of the land.) Tj T*
(The Pasha having set the example himself, strongly recommended all his) Tj T*
(ministers to have their children educated in like manner; and I have) Tj T*
