() Tj T*
(Of course during my stay in Egypt, I had often opportunities of visiting) Tj T*
(all the known antiquities, and amongst others the celebrated Pyramids,) Tj T*
(those noble testimonies of the bygone splendour of the land, and whose) Tj T*
(age and founders seem destined ever to remain a mystery.  A friend of) Tj T*
(mine, a great antiquarian, and one deeply read in profane and sacred) Tj T*
(history, used to delight in holding forth to me his speculations as to) Tj T*
(their origin.  His opinion was, that it must be erroneous to imagine that) Tj T*
(these pyramids were the handiwork of the Israelites.  In support of this) Tj T*
(argument he quoted from many authorities, and amongst others from a) Tj T*
(well-known traveller who saw at one place the people making bricks with) Tj T*
(straw cut into small pieces, mingled with the clay to bind it.  Hence it) Tj T*
(is, that when villages built of this brick fall into decay, the roads are) Tj T*
(full of small particles of straw, extremely offensive to the eyes in a) Tj T*
(high wind.  These persons were engaged, exactly as the Israelites used to) Tj T*
(be, making bricks with straw, and for a similar purpose, viz? to build) Tj T*
(extensive granaries for the Pasha??_treasure cities for Pharaoh_.?  Hence) Tj T*
(my friend argued that the Israelites laboured in making bricks, not in) Tj T*
(hewing stones such as the pyramids are constructed with; but I do not) Tj T*
(pretend to enter into any argument upon so learned and obscure a subject:) Tj T*
(I certainly was surprised at the magnificence of their structure, and) Tj T*
(often wondered within myself where the stones came from, by what means) Tj T*
