(With regard to the climate of Egypt, I believe it to be as good as many) Tj T*
(parts of Syria, though the heat is certainly more intense, and even I) Tj T*
(myself suffered from languor and oppression; but then the mornings and) Tj T*
(evenings fully recompense you for the sultrier heat of the day, and I) Tj T*
(never recollect to have enjoyed a summer?s moonlight night more than I) Tj T*
(did upon the Nile.  The European residents in general enjoy excellent) Tj T*
(health; and few that have resided there long would wish to change their) Tj T*
(method of living, or the country they live in.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In Cairo, the Consular Square contains many very handsome buildings,) Tj T*
(inhabited principally by the consuls of various nations, and some of the) Tj T*
(more wealthy European merchants.  With my friend Mr. Walne, the British) Tj T*
(Consul at Cairo, I have spent many a pleasant hour, and for his great) Tj T*
(kindness and hospitality, I am glad to have an opportunity of thus) Tj T*
(publicly thanking him.  Mr. W. is the head of the Egyptian Society, who) Tj T*
(have a very fine library, consisting chiefly of works relating to the) Tj T*
(antiquities and country of Egypt.  The valuable books contained in this) Tj T*
(library are at all times, with perfect goodwill, placed at the disposal) Tj T*
(of strangers; and I gratefully acknowledge having derived useful) Tj T*
(information and amusement from the well-stocked shelves of this) Tj T*
(institution.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A great source of comfort to English families residing in Egypt, is the) Tj T*
(punctual regularity with which the European mails arrive and depart; for,) Tj T*
(besides meeting almost weekly with swarms of their countrymen and fair) Tj T*
(countrywomen flocking to and from India, they have constantly fresh news) Tj T*
(from home, and can, upon any great emergency, transport themselves from) Tj T*
(the warm clime of Egypt to their own much-loved foggy island within the) Tj T*
(fortnight.  Besides this, they are continually receiving newspapers from) Tj T*
(all parts of the world possessing the advantage over England of being) Tj T*
(cognisant of Indian and Australian news a fortnight before such) Tj T*
(intelligence could reach London; and this for merchants connected by) Tj T*
(trade with both places, must naturally be of paramount importance.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(During winter, the Europeans at Cairo are much given to festivities;) Tj T*
(dinner-parties and balls and soirées are then the order of the day, and) Tj T*
(great good feeling exists amongst the residents.  Even private) Tj T*
(theatricals have been attempted; but it is during the Carnival that Cairo) Tj T*
(resounds with merriment, and masques and grotesque-looking figures, with) Tj T*
(torches and music, parade the streets from house to house till long after) Tj T*
(midnight, few enjoying the fun better than the native Cairines) Tj T*
(themselves.  The gentlemen have shooting parties and coursing matches;) Tj T*
(the ladies ride out in the environs; they have healthy exercise, good) Tj T*
(houses, and the best of fare?all the productions of the East blended with) Tj T*
(the luxuries imported from European markets; and in this respect, as well) Tj T*
