(CHAPTER XII.) Tj T*
(VISITS TO LADY ROLLE AND TO BATH AND CHELTENHAM.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Engaged in completing my manuscript preparatory to publication, I had) Tj T*
(devoted myself unweariedly to the task, and was about to correct the few) Tj T*
(last pages, when I was hindered by an invitation to pay a visit at Bicton) Tj T*
(in Devonshire, and there to recruit my health a little after my labour.) Tj T*
(Having accepted it, I purpose, for the present, to give a short account) Tj T*
(of my visit there; also to Bath and Cheltenham, which afforded me great) Tj T*
(pleasure, and which I hope will interest my readers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Lady Rolle had kindly invited me to visit her at Merton, which invitation) Tj T*
(I was very glad to accept; and I left London by an express train in full) Tj T*
(anticipation of much enjoyment.  I had often heard the gardens at Bicton) Tj T*
(described as amongst the finest in this county; no pen, however, can do) Tj T*
(justice to their attractions, and the loveliness of the surrounding) Tj T*
(scenery, which burst forth in all the majesty of a warm spring day,) Tj T*
(agreeably contrasting with the dark and murky atmosphere of London.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The rapidity of travelling by an express train really seems magical.  If) Tj T*
(I were to write to my friends in the East and tell them I had travelled) Tj T*
