(the weather was lovely, and forcibly brought to my mind the contrast) Tj T*
(between the murky and ungenial atmosphere which pervaded London when I) Tj T*
(left it, and the bright clear air of this favoured portion of England.) Tj T*
(Could my readers, who spend so much of their time in the metropolis, have) Tj T*
(felt as I did on this morning, when the sweet breeze, wafting the odours) Tj T*
(of the fresh-turned earth, seemed to breathe health upon the cheek, and) Tj T*
(purity and peace into the heart, they could never again declare that the) Tj T*
(country possessed no charms.  Contemplate but the rising of day?s bright) Tj T*
(luminary, which in the west of England is especially glorious, making its) Tj T*
(appearance as it does from behind lofty and undulating lines of hills,) Tj T*
(overlooking the loveliest of valleys, which must in spring present more) Tj T*
(the appearance of a Syrian glen than anything I have hitherto seen.  The) Tj T*
(verdant moss, the delicate white violet, and the modest primrose, which) Tj T*
(hid their loveliness beneath a variety of trees, and amongst them the) Tj T*
(first that puts forth its blossoms is the sallow, whose yellow downy buds) Tj T*
(emit a honeyed odour, all combine to constitute this beautiful part of) Tj T*
(England a very Garden of Eden in which an humble mind might dwell for) Tj T*
(ever.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The impression produced on my mind by these scenes, was very similar to) Tj T*
(that which so painfully affects the Swiss, when in a foreign country he) Tj T*
(is reminded of his wild and mountainous home.  I felt all the sensations) Tj T*
(of the indescribable ?mal de pays.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But I must proceed on my journey.  I entered the railway carriage, and) Tj T*
(quick as lightning sped from all those who had shewn me so much kindness) Tj T*
(and attention, and to whom I shall often travel back in thought to dwell) Tj T*
(with grateful satisfaction and delight on this happy period of my life.) Tj T*
(Should any of my readers, who have not yet visited Bath, have occasion) Tj T*
(hereafter to do so, they will not fail, as I was, to be struck with the) Tj T*
(picturesque appearance which meets the eye just before arriving at this) Tj T*
(beautiful city; the numerous pretty meadows?the spires of churches rising) Tj T*
(here and there to remind the beholder that he is in a Christian) Tj T*
