(CHAPTER XVII.) Tj T*
(CHRISTIAN INHABITANTS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among the Christian inhabitants of Syria, the Maronites, in point of) Tj T*
(numbers, if not in the simplicity of their faith, certainly take rank) Tj T*
(next to the devout followers of the Orthodox Eastern Church, and the) Tj T*
(brief review I propose to take of their history and position will, I) Tj T*
(think, sufficiently establish for them a claim to be placed among the) Tj T*
(most interesting Christian races or nations which can be found in any) Tj T*
(part of the globe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To the present hour they continue to inhabit the mountains of Lebanon and) Tj T*
(Anti-Lebanon, in which twelve centuries since they sought and found) Tj T*
(refuge from the decided measures to which the general Council of) Tj T*
(Constantinople had recourse, in order to punish them for their adherence) Tj T*
(to the Monothelite heresy.  Driven from their homes in the plains and) Tj T*
(cities of the land, they established themselves in perfect security in) Tj T*
(the mountain fastnesses, which have enabled them on more than one) Tj T*
(occasion to set the power of the Egyptian and Turkish Governments at) Tj T*
(defiance, and to afford to others, no matter what their faith or origin,) Tj T*
(an impenetrable asylum against the persecutions of their enemies.) Tj T*
(Europeans or Easterns, Christians or infidels, flying before the) Tj T*
(persecutions of political or religious bigots, are still received with) Tj T*
(open arms and untiring hospitality by the Maronites, whose forefathers) Tj T*
(always practised the virtues learned in adversity?virtues which they have) Tj T*
(most successfully inculcated on the minds of their descendants.  No) Tj T*
(greater proof than this can be brought forward of the excellence of their) Tj T*
(principles, their courage and integrity of heart, since even from that) Tj T*
(early period they made Lebanon what Hebron and other ancient cities were) Tj T*
(among the children of Israel.  The extraordinary liberality and) Tj T*
(hospitality displayed by the original inhabitants can alone account for) Tj T*
(the striking amalgamation of Christian and unbelieving races, and for) Tj T*
(their having inhabited the mountains, for so long a period, in perfect) Tj T*
(amity and good-will towards each other, except when bad feelings have) Tj T*
(been excited by the intrigues or intermeddling of the foreign powers,) Tj T*
