() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_Kindly communicated to me by my friend_, _Professor Edward Forbes_.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(MY DEAR SIR,?It is much to be desired that a careful geological) Tj T*
(exploration of your interesting country should be undertaken by an able) Tj T*
(investigator.  All that we know of the structure of Syria is fragmentary,) Tj T*
(and in great part unsatisfactory.  Sufficient, however, is known to) Tj T*
(indicate the scientific importance of the region, and to hold out a) Tj T*
(promise of a rich harvest for the practical geologist who may undertake) Tj T*
(its description.  The collection of fossils which I have myself seen from) Tj T*
(the district around Lebanon, suggested many enquiries that have not yet) Tj T*
(been answered, especially respecting the relations of the jurassic and) Tj T*
(cretaceous rocks of that famous region.  The following scanty notices of) Tj T*
(what is known about Syrian formations and their fossils, may serve to) Tj T*
(excite curiosity and to direct the traveller to fresh observations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the year 1833, a valuable memoir by M. Botta, Jun., was published by) Tj T*
(the Geological Society of France.  It is entitled ?Observations sur le) Tj T*
(Liban et l?Antiliban.?  He represents Mount Lebanon as composed of rocks) Tj T*
(belonging to the lower cretaceous series, resting upon green sands, and) Tj T*
(these in their turn reposing upon jurassic strata.  He states, that in) Tj T*
(the chain of the Lebanon there are three distinct formations.  The) Tj T*
(uppermost is a limestone, very variable in character, both of appearance) Tj T*
(and hardness, and alternating with calcareous marls.  The lower division) Tj T*
(of this formation is distinguished by the presence of beds and nodules of) Tj T*
(flint.  Fossil sea-urchins occur in its middle, and fishes in its lower) Tj T*
(part.  A second formation of variable thickness is sandy, very) Tj T*
(ferruginous, abounding in iron ores and lignites, and passing above into) Tj T*
(a calcareous rock.  The lowest formation is constituted of numerous beds) Tj T*
(of cavernous limestone.  Besides these older rocks, M. Botta remarks upon) Tj T*
(the presence, all along the coast from Beyrout to Tripoli, of) Tj T*
(conglomerates or sandstones, quite unconformable to the calcareous rocks) Tj T*
(of the coast.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
